<newsitem itemid="116570" id="root" date="1996-10-15" xml:lang="en">
<title>SOUTH AFRICA: Pik 'n Pay half year earnings rise.</title>
<headline>Pik 'n Pay half year earnings rise.</headline>
<dateline>JOHANNESBURG 1996-10-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>Half year period</p>
<p>			(million rand unless stated)</p>
<p>    Shr, attrib (cts)     10.37	 vs 8.25</p>
<p>    Div (cts)		 3.80	  vs 2.92</p>
<p>    Attrib income	   48.6	  vs 38.7</p>
<p>    Pre-tax		   82.0	  vs 70.2</p>
<p>    Tax			 29.1	  vs 26.2</p>
<p>    Interest paid	    8.8	  vs  8.4</p>
<p>    Interest received     27.2	  vs 16.3</p>
<p>    Exceptional items	3.2	  vs nil</p>
<p>    Turnover	     4,618.8	  vs 4,400.4</p>
<p>Div payable Nov 14</p>
<p>on register Nov 1</p>
<p>Full name: Pick 'n Pay Stores Ltd</p>
<p>NOTE - Analysts polled by Reuters had forecast first half earnings per share of 9.5-11.0 cents for the retailer, with the consensus at 10.1, and a dividend of 3.5-4.2, with the consensus at 3.7 cents.</p>
<p>The exceptional items consist of the write-off of goodwill and trade marks on buying a further 25 percent in Score Supermarkets and 75 percent of PNA Stationers, less the profit from the sale of PriceClub.</p>
<p>-- Cape Town newsroom +27 21 25-2238</p>
</text>
